  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A Heat Advisory remains in effect today for parts \nof the Triangle and south-central NC, and we've issued another Heat \nAdvisory for the Triangle to the S and E for Mon. The hot weather \nwill continue into mid week, will gradually lowering heat indices \nmid through late week. \n\nHeating has been curbed somewhat today by this morning's areas of \nlow clouds as well as the early-onset of convection across parts of \nthe Sandhills and far N CWA. But several locations have still \nmanaged to reach a 105F heat index today, as pockets of strong \nheating combine with high dewpoints in the mid-upper 70s. We should \nsee a bit less cloudiness Mon morning, and with low level \nthicknesses expected to reach 20 m above normal, highs should reach \nthe mid 90s to near 100F. The small bit of good news is that the \ndewpoints tomorrow are expected to be slightly lower than today, \ndipping into the 70-76 range with mixing in the afternoon, with \nthese lower readings in the west, and this should help keep heat \nindices from reaching criteria for an Extreme Heat Warning, although \ncertainly this will be assessed further tonight and monitored \nclosely on Mon. But a Heat Advisory is warranted for all but NW and \nVA border portions of the CWA, with heat index values expected to \nreach 105-109F. NWS Heat Risk, which is based on how unusual and/or \nlong-lived this heat is, as well as on past CDC heat-illness data, \nreaches a level 3 (out of 4) nearly everywhere Mon, suggesting that \nthis heat could be particularly dangerous. \n\nTemps may be a bit less hot on Tue, but still elevated over the S \nand E CWA, where the long duration of this hot stretch will add to \nthe already-high heat stress. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The threat for a few strong to severe storms \ncontinues through Mon. We have a Marginal (level 1 of 5) risk of \nsevere thunderstorms across the northern half of central NC for the \nrest of today, and a Slight (level 2 of 5) risk in the Piedmont on \nMon. Damaging winds are the primary threat.\n\nA few strong to severe storms remain possible today and Mon. Deep \nlayer bulk shear remains poor today, under 20 kts, which will limit \nthe intensity and duration of any updraft. But SBCAPE has reached \n2500-4000 J/kg thus far (according to RAP-based mesoanalysis) with a \nDCAPE over 800 J/kg much of the area, highest SW, and with PWs still \naround 2\" or up to 130%-140% of normal, isolated water-loaded \ndowndrafts remain possible, leading to localized strong to severe \ngusts into early this evening. On Mon, the SBCAPE may be a bit \nlower, but our deep layer shear is expected to be around 25 kts, \nwith added forcing for ascent provided by an approaching MCV \nexpected later in the day from the NW. In particular, convection \nover the NC mountains may maintain strength with better organization \nas they track ESE into the NC Piedmont, again with damaging winds as \nthe primary threat, from mid afternoon through mid evening. \n\nStorm coverage is expected to decrease and push to our SE sections \non Tue, as a slug of drier air with lower PW spreads in from the WNW.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Heat will linger through Thu, followed by more- \nseasonable temperatures and above average probabilities of \nrain/convection late week through at least the first half of the \nweekend. \n\nA sub-tropical ridge now over TX will retrograde into the Southwest \nand Four Corners while amplifying sharply through the Rockies \nthrough the week, while a downstream trough will amplify across the \nern US. Although the models strongly indicate a cyclone will develop \nwithin that trough over the Great Lakes and vicinity by the end of \nthe weekend, there remains large model spread in its depth and how \nquickly it will lift through ern Canada through early next week. \nRelated falling heights, and moist and unseasonably strong swly flow \neast of the trough axis, will favor both less hot temperatures and \nalso above average probabilities of convection, some probably \nsevere, late Thu-Sat.",
